cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Detecting human presence with Wi-Fi signal strength on a
  Raspberry Pi 3B
message: >-
  If you use this software or data, please cite it using the
  metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Aryan
    family-names: Natekar
repository-code: "https://github.com/AryanNatekar1/wifi-presence-sensing"
url: "https://aryannatekar1.github.io/wifi-presence-sensing/"
abstract: >-
  An experimental study of whether Received Signal Strength
  Indicator (RSSI) measurements from commodity Raspberry Pi
  hardware can detect human movement, together with the software
  used to collect, analyse and visualise the data. Against a
  threshold fixed before the controlled trials, short-window RSSI
  variance separated an empty room from continuous human movement
  by a factor of 5.7 to 6.1 with non-overlapping distributions.
  The study also documents three experimental confounds - USB
  undervoltage, insufficient transmitter-receiver separation, and
  inconsistent door state - each of which produced convincing but
  incorrect results before being identified. All raw traces,
  including the void and confounded trials, are published so that
  every reported figure can be recomputed.
keywords:
  - wi-fi sensing
  - RSSI
  - presence detection
  - raspberry pi
  - signal processing
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
date-released: "2026-09-14"
